Feature: enrolling certificates with ML-DSA keys

  As a user
  I want to enroll certificates with post-quantum ML-DSA keys, whether the key and CSR are generated locally by vcert,
  provided by me, or generated by the service

  The NGTSmldsa endpoint needs NGTS_ZONE_MLDSA, an issuing template that allows ML-DSA 44, 65 and 87. Service generated
  keys also need an active VSatellite in the NGTS tenant.

  Background:
    And the default aruba exit timeout is 180 seconds

  @COMMON
  Scenario Outline: where it generates an ML-DSA CSR
    When I generate random CSR with flags -key-type mldsa -key-size <strength> -no-prompt -csr-file csr.pem -key-file k.pem
    Then it should write CSR to the file named "csr.pem"
      And it should write private key to the file named "k.pem"
      And "csr.pem" should be a CSR with an ML-DSA-<strength> public key

    Examples:
      | strength |
      | 44       |
      | 65       |
      | 87       |

  Scenario Outline: where it enrolls a certificate with an ML-DSA key generated locally
    When I enroll random certificate using <endpoint> with -csr local -key-type mldsa -key-size <strength> -no-prompt -cert-file c.pem -chain-file ch.pem -key-file k.pem
    Then it should post certificate request
      And it should retrieve certificate
      And "c.pem" should be a certificate with an ML-DSA-<strength> public key
      And private key in "k.pem" and certificate in "c.pem" should have the same public key

    @NGTS
    Examples:
      | endpoint  | strength |
      | NGTSmldsa | 44       |
      | NGTSmldsa | 65       |
      | NGTSmldsa | 87       |

  Scenario Outline: where it enrolls a certificate with an ML-DSA CSR generated beforehand
    Given I generate random CSR with flags -key-type mldsa -key-size <strength> -no-prompt -csr-file csr.pem -key-file k.pem
      And it should write CSR to the file named "csr.pem"
    When I enroll a certificate using <endpoint> with -csr file:csr.pem -no-pickup
      And it should post certificate request
      And I retrieve the certificate from <endpoint> using the same Pickup ID with -cert-file c.pem -chain-file ch.pem -timeout 190
    Then it should retrieve certificate
      And it should not output private key
      And "c.pem" should be a certificate with an ML-DSA-<strength> public key
      And CSR in "csr.pem" and certificate in "c.pem" should have the same public key
      And private key in "k.pem" and certificate in "c.pem" should have the same public key

    @NGTS
    Examples:
      | endpoint  | strength |
      | NGTSmldsa | 44       |
      | NGTSmldsa | 65       |
      | NGTSmldsa | 87       |

  Scenario Outline: where it enrolls a certificate with an ML-DSA key generated by the service
    When I enroll random certificate with dummy password using <endpoint> with -csr service -key-type mldsa -key-size <strength> -no-prompt -cert-file c.pem -chain-file ch.pem -key-file k.pem
    Then it should post certificate request
      And it should retrieve certificate
      And "c.pem" should be a certificate with an ML-DSA-<strength> public key
      And "k.pem" should be an encrypted PKCS#8 private key
      And private key in "k.pem" encrypted with dummy password and certificate in "c.pem" should have the same public key

    @NGTS
    Examples:
      | endpoint  | strength |
      | NGTSmldsa | 44       |
      | NGTSmldsa | 65       |
      | NGTSmldsa | 87       |

  Scenario Outline: where it rejects an ML-DSA request without a strength
    When I enroll random certificate using <endpoint> with -csr <csr> -key-type mldsa -no-prompt
    Then the exit status should not be 0
      And the output should contain "'--key-type mldsa' requires '--key-size' to be one of [44 65 87]"

    @FAKE
    Examples:
      | endpoint  | csr     |
      | test-mode | local   |
      | test-mode | service |

  Scenario Outline: where it rejects the legacy PEM format for ML-DSA keys
    When I enroll random certificate using <endpoint> with -csr <csr> -key-type mldsa -key-size 65 -no-prompt -format legacy-pem
    Then the exit status should not be 0
      And the output should contain "the 'legacy-pem' format cannot be used with '--key-type mldsa'"

    @FAKE
    Examples:
      | endpoint  | csr     |
      | test-mode | local   |
      | test-mode | service |
